The core form, verified:

```tsx
const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors, isSubmitting } } = useForm<FormData>({
  resolver: zodResolver(schema),   // optional: schema validation
  defaultValues: { email: '', qty: 1 },
});

<form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onValid, onInvalid)}>
  <input {...register('email', {
    required: 'Email is required',
    pattern: { value: /.+@.+\..+/, message: 'Enter a valid email' },
  })} />
  {errors.email && <p className="error">{errors.email.message}</p>}

  <button disabled={isSubmitting}>Submit</button>
</form>

// the handler only ever sees VALID data — RHF ran the gate
const onValid = (data: FormData) => api.save(data);
```

Why it doesn't re-render on typing (the interview core): register returns ref/onChange handlers wired to RHF's internal store — the input updates the store through the ref, validation runs in the store, and NOTHING in React state changes per keystroke. The component re-renders ONLY when something you render from formState changes — errors.email appearing on blur, isSubmitting flipping. That's the whole performance model: ~0 renders per keystroke vs Formik's whole-form re-render each character.

The three APIs in one sentence each: register = 'wire this field' (ref + rules + change tracking); handleSubmit = 'validate everything, then call my fn with typed data or fill errors' (it's curried: handleSubmit(onValid)(event) is what onSubmit actually receives); formState = the reactive surface — errors, isDirty, touchedFields, isSubmitting — read through the proxy so RHF knows WHICH parts you use and re-renders only for those.

Controller for the escape cases (say when asked): <Controller name="notes" control={control} render={({ field }) => <RichTextEditor value={field.value} onChange={field.onChange} />} /> — for components that MUST be controlled (no ref support, value/onChange API only, like MUI/Chakra inputs). Controller re-renders per keystroke like any controlled input — use it ONLY where register genuinely can't work.
